  <h3>Starting With User Empathy</h3>
  <p>Before designing any AI system, understand who uses it, what problems they face, and why current solutions fail. This empathy stage is critical but often skipped by engineers eager to build.</p>

  <p>For AI products, understanding the full ecosystem matters: end users, data teams, ML engineers, product managers, and compliance officers all have different needs. Your architecture must serve all these stakeholders.</p>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Enterprise AI:</strong> A recommendation system might have 95% accuracy, but if it cannot explain its reasoning to business users, they won't trust it. Empathy reveals this gap early.</p>
    <p><strong>In Regulated Industries:</strong> Healthcare AI must address integration with clinician workflows, regulatory compliance, and liability. Users care about explainability and auditability.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Design thinking centers on user needs, not engineering capabilities</li>
      <li>Empathy involves understanding the full stakeholder ecosystem</li>
      <li>Iteration is fundamental—return to earlier stages as you learn</li>
      <li>Success metrics must come from user needs, not model benchmarks</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>🧪 Test Your Understanding</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="1">
      <p>You build a medical AI model with 98% accuracy, but clinicians find it too slow. What stage of design thinking did you skip?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>B) Empathize—you didn't understand clinical workflows</li>
        <li>A) Define—you didn't frame the problem</li>
        <li>C) Prototype—you should have tested earlier</li>
        <li>D) Test—you didn't validate with real users</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">B is correct. You skipped empathy by not understanding time constraints in clinical environments. This is a classic example of optimizing for technical metrics instead of user experience.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
