<section class="section reveal" id="problem-framing">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>From Insights to Problem Statements</h2>

  <p>After understanding users, define the problem clearly. A poorly defined problem leads to building the wrong system, optimizing for the wrong metrics, and missing user needs.</p>

  <p>A strong problem statement for AI has four components: a specific user, a clear need, the current barrier, and measurable success criteria. These must be grounded in actual user research, not assumptions.</p>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Strong vs. Weak Problem Statements</div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>❌ Weak Statement</h5>
        <p>"We need AI to improve customer satisfaction."</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Too vague—which customers?</li>
          <li>No measurement—improve how much?</li>
          <li>No user perspective</li>
          <li>No clear barrier to solve</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>✅ Strong Statement</h5>
        <p>"Support agents spend 30% of time searching the knowledge base. We need AI to rank articles in less than 3 seconds, reducing search time by 50%."</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Specific user: support agents</li>
          <li>Measurable need: 30% of time</li>
          <li>Clear barrier: slow search, poor ranking</li>
          <li>Success metric: 3s latency, 50% reduction</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Metrics That Matter</h3>
  <p>Most AI projects optimize for model metrics (accuracy, F1 score, NDCG) and ignore product metrics (adoption, latency, user satisfaction). The best systems align these metrics so that improving model performance actually improves user outcomes.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Metrics Hierarchy</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># User Need → Product Metric → Model Metric</span>

<span class="cm"># User Need (from empathy)</span>
<span class="st">"Find relevant articles quickly"</span>

<span class="cm"># Product Metrics (what you measure)</span>
<span class="va">avg_search_time</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"< 3 seconds"</span>
<span class="va">adoption_rate</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"70% of searches"</span>
<span class="va">agent_satisfaction</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"NPS > 40"</span>

<span class="cm"># Model Metrics (technical validation)</span>
<span class="va">precision_at_1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">">= 0.8"</span>
<span class="va">latency_p99</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"< 2 seconds"</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> Product metrics (adoption, latency) come first. If users don't adopt because the system is slow, model metrics are irrelevant. Always work backwards from user needs.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Problem statements must be specific: user, need, barrier, success metric</li>
      <li>Product metrics matter more than model metrics early on</li>
      <li>Validate that improving model metrics improves user outcomes</li>
      <li>Success criteria should be quantitative and measurable</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

