<section class="section reveal" id="advanced-topics">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced</span>
  <h2>Advanced Techniques and Optimization Strategies</h2>

  <p>As your systems mature, you'll encounter challenges that require deeper expertise. This section covers advanced patterns used by organizations running massive-scale AI systems.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Example configuration for production deployments</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: ai-system-config
data:
  enable_observability: <span class="st">"true"</span>
  metric_interval: <span class="st">"30s"</span>
  alert_threshold: <span class="st">"95"</span>
  enable_tracing: <span class="st">"true"</span>
  sampling_rate: <span class="st">"0.1"</span>
  log_level: <span class="st">"INFO"</span>
  retention_days: <span class="st">"30"</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> Production configurations define behavior across all environments. Sampling rate controls what percentage of traces are recorded. Alert thresholds should be tuned to your SLOs, not arbitrary values. Log retention balances compliance requirements with storage costs.</p>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Challenge 1: Scaling Observability</strong> — When you have thousands of services generating millions of metrics per second, storage becomes expensive. Solutions include better compression, sampling strategies, and tiered storage.</p>

  <p><strong>Challenge 2: Cost Management</strong> — Cloud observability services charge per metric, per log line, and per trace. Large-scale systems can spend hundreds of thousands per month. Effective cardinality management and sampling strategies are essential.</p>

  <p><strong>Challenge 3: Alert Tuning</strong> — Too many alerts cause alert fatigue. Too few and you miss real issues. The solution is iterative tuning based on incident analysis: when incidents occur, tune alerts to catch them; when false alerts fire, adjust thresholds.</p>

