<section class="section reveal" id="summary">
  <span class="section-label">Wrap-Up</span>
  <h2>Summary and Key Takeaways</h2>

  <p>In this lesson, you've learned the foundations of building observability systems that enable production excellence. The core principles:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Observability is Architectural</strong> — Design it in from day one; retrofitting is expensive and incomplete</li>
    <li><strong>Three Pillars Enable Root-Cause Analysis</strong> — Metrics show trends, logs show details, traces show flows</li>
    <li><strong>AI Systems Need AI-Specific Metrics</strong> — Model accuracy, confidence, data drift are as important as infrastructure metrics</li>
    <li><strong>Start Simple, Scale Deliberately</strong> — Begin with essential metrics and dashboards; expand based on needs</li>
    <li><strong>Close the Feedback Loop</strong> — Incidents should drive observability improvements; use postmortems to iterate</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Production AI systems require continuous attention to observability. The teams that excel at observability respond faster to incidents, ship features confidently, and build systems that customers trust.</p>

  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>🧪 Final Understanding Check</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="3">
      <p>You're building a new AI service. When should you design your observability strategy?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) After the MVP is ready to ship</li>
        <li>B) When the first incident occurs</li>
        <li>C) When you move to production</li>
        <li>D) During the architectural design phase, before implementation</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">The answer is D. Observability must be designed alongside architecture, not bolted on afterward. During design, you decide what's critical to measure, how you'll aggregate data, and how you'll alert. Building observability from day one is cheaper and more effective than retrofitting it later.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

